                <h2>Comprehensive Care for Skin Changes</h2>
                <p>Rashes and skin discoloration can be caused by a wide variety of conditions, from allergic reactions to chronic skin diseases. At Smart Skin Dermatology, our board-certified dermatologists are experts in identifying the cause of your skin changes and providing effective treatment.</p>
                
                <h3>Types of Rashes We Treat</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Contact Dermatitis:</strong> Allergic or irritant reactions from soaps, cosmetics, plants, or metals</li>
                    <li><strong>Hives (Urticaria):</strong> Raised, itchy welts that appear suddenly</li>
                    <li><strong>Rosacea:</strong> Facial redness, flushing, and sometimes small bumps</li>
                    <li><strong>Seborrheic Dermatitis:</strong> Scaly, flaky patches often on the scalp and face</li>
                    <li><strong>Drug Rashes:</strong> Reactions to medications</li>
                    <li><strong>Heat Rash:</strong> Blocked sweat ducts causing small bumps</li>
                    <li><strong>Viral Rashes:</strong> Rashes accompanying viral infections</li>
                    <li><strong>Fungal Infections:</strong> Ringworm, athlete's foot, and yeast infections</li>
                </ul>
                
                <h3>Skin Discoloration Conditions</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Melasma:</strong> Brown or gray-brown patches, often on the face</li>
                    <li><strong>Vitiligo:</strong> Loss of skin pigment causing white patches</li>
                    <li><strong>Post-Inflammatory Hyperpigmentation:</strong> Dark spots after acne or injury</li>
                    <li><strong>Age Spots (Solar Lentigines):</strong> Sun-induced brown spots</li>
                    <li><strong>Birthmarks:</strong> Including café-au-lait spots and port-wine stains</li>
                    <li><strong>Tinea Versicolor:</strong> Fungal infection causing lighter or darker patches</li>
                </ul>
                
                <h3>Our Diagnostic Approach</h3>
                <p>Accurate diagnosis is crucial for effective treatment. Our evaluation may include:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Detailed medical history review</li>
                    <li>Visual examination with dermoscopy</li>
                    <li>Patch testing for allergies</li>
                    <li>Skin scraping or culture for infections</li>
                    <li>Skin biopsy when needed for definitive diagnosis</li>
                </ul>
                
                <h3>Treatment Options</h3>
                <p>Based on your diagnosis, treatment may include:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Topical Medications:</strong> Corticosteroids, antifungals, antibiotics, or retinoids</li>
                    <li><strong>Oral Medications:</strong> Antihistamines, antibiotics, or immunosuppressants</li>
                    <li><strong>Phototherapy:</strong> Light-based treatments for certain conditions</li>
                    <li><strong>Laser Treatments:</strong> For pigmentation and vascular issues</li>
                    <li><strong>Chemical Peels:</strong> For hyperpigmentation and melasma</li>
                    <li><strong>Allergen Avoidance:</strong> Identifying and avoiding triggers</li>
                </ul>
                
                <h3>When to Seek Immediate Care</h3>
                <p>Contact us right away or seek emergency care if you experience:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Rash accompanied by fever or difficulty breathing</li>
                    <li>Rapidly spreading rash</li>
                    <li>Blisters or peeling skin</li>
                    <li>Signs of infection (pus, warmth, spreading redness)</li>
                    <li>Rash affecting eyes, mouth, or genitals</li>
                </ul>
